sections.say:
"Two structural pieces. First, fixed-width zero-padded labels — `String(n).padStart(6, '0')` — so that lex sort matches numeric sort. Critical because document-production tooling sorts Bates numbers as strings: `'EVE000010'` must sort AFTER `'EVE000002'`, which only works if both are zero-padded. Without padding, `'EVE10' < 'EVE2'` lex, which breaks every downstream tool.

"Second, the off-by-one. A doc with `pages = N` starting at `start` spans `start..start+N-1`, NOT `start..start+N`. Five pages starting at 4 spans 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — end is 8, which is `4 + 5 - 1`. The next doc starts at `end + 1 = 9`. Forgetting the `-1` shifts every subsequent doc by one, which corrupts the entire production.

```
let next = 1;
for (const d of docs) {
  const start = next;
  const end = next + d.pages - 1;     // off-by-one — pages-1, not pages
  const range = d.pages === 1 ? label(start) : label(start) + '-' + label(end);
  out.push(`${d.name}: ${range}`);
  next = end + 1;
}
```

"And the 1-page special case — emit a single label, not a degenerate range like `EVE000003-EVE000003`. That's not a performance optimization; it's matching real legal-citation convention."

reference.code:
function bates(docs, prefix = 'EVE', width = 6) {
  // Fixed-width zero-padded labels sort and collate correctly in a legal production.
  const label = n => prefix + String(n).padStart(width, '0');
  const out = [];
  let next = 1;
  // The counter is stateful across docs — can't be a pure .map.
  for (const d of docs) {
    const start = next;
    const end = next + d.pages - 1; // off-by-one: a 1-page doc spans start..start, not start..start+1
    out.push(`${d.name}: ${d.pages === 1 ? label(start) : label(start) + '-' + label(end)}`);
    next = end + 1;
  }
  return out;
}
const docs = [{ name: 'Intake', pages: 2 }, { name: 'X-Ray', pages: 1 }, { name: 'ER Report', pages: 5 }];
bates(docs).forEach(l => console.log(l));

